 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; the quiz in <a href="#two">Two ways to guide a weapon</a> and Figures 2 to 4 cover more.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Your target is a vehicle that may drive off, under clear skies. Laser or coordinates?</summary><p>Laser, or a dual-mode weapon: a laser spot can follow a moving target, while a single-mode coordinate weapon still flies to the coordinates and misses if the target moves.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Why might a GPS weapon be the better choice through thick haze?</summary><p>It needs no designator and no sight of the target, so obscurants do not affect it; a laser can be absorbed or scattered by them.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> The laser target line is 140. Is a final attack heading window of 080 to 115 acceptable?</summary><p>Yes. Every heading in it is 25° to 60° off the line: inside the 120° attack zone and clear of the 20° safety zone. 100 is in the preferred 10° to 45°.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Why is attacking along the laser target line's own heading avoided?</summary><p>You would be in the safety zone, behind the designator, where the seeker may lock onto the designator or the scatter near it instead of the target.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Your aircraft has no designator. Can you still drop laser-guided bombs?</summary><p>Yes, with assisted designation: another aircraft (buddy lasing), a FAC(A) or a ground team lases for you, on your bombs' code.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Who corrects it if you check in with a different code than the controller expected?</summary><p>The controller: making the corrections when an aircraft checks in with a different code is the controller's responsibility.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> You called SPOT, CEASE LASER. Can you attack?</summary><p>Not yet. SPOT and CAPTURED are not the same: the spot may be slightly off the target, so the controller confirms what your sensor is on before you call CAPTURED.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Smoke from the first impact drifts over the target area. What can the designator do?</summary><p>Reposition to the flank or high ground, hand the mission to a laser that is not obscured, and work further targets from downwind to upwind.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> You are given coordinates for a bomb-on-coordinate attack and your pod shows the target a little to one side. Do you adjust them?</summary><p>No. Aircrew do not adjust passed coordinates for any reason; that is why the lowest-error coordinates must be obtained and entered correctly.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> What does CE90 of 40 ft mean, and which category is it?</summary><p>There is a 90 percent chance the target is within 40 ft horizontally of the coordinates: CAT II.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Two units use different datums for the same coordinates. Why does it matter?</summary><p>The same numbers then mark different places on the ground, which can cause significant misses and endanger friendly troops inside the error.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-jp"><strong>[1] US Joint Chiefs of Staff, JP 3-09.3, Close Air Support, 25 November 2014.</strong> <a href="https://www.bits.de/NRANEU/others/jp-doctrine/jp3_09_3(14).pdf">Read on the Berlin Information-center for Transatlantic Security's copy</a>, 5 November 2026, by printed page: terminal guidance and unmanned aircraft pp. I-2 and I-6; FAC(A) targeting pods p. II-8; IAMs and datums p. III-28; targeting and target location error pp. III-33 to III-35; laser-guided and inertially aided munitions pp. III-52 to III-62; laser marks and brevity p. III-79; simultaneous attacks p. V-5; third-party lasing p. V-22; laser hand-off pp. V-35 to V-36; example pp. E-23 to E-24. A later edition may differ.</p>
 <p id="source-usna"><strong>[2] US Naval Academy, Weapons and Systems Engineering Department, Fundamentals of Naval Weapons Systems, chapters 10 and 15.</strong> Read on the Federation of American Scientists' copies of <a href="https://man.fas.org/dod-101/navy/docs/fun/part10.htm">chapter 10, Visible and Infrared Spectrum</a> (10.6.2.3 divergence, 10.6.3.3 laser target designators) and <a href="https://man.fas.org/dod-101/navy/docs/fun/part15.htm">chapter 15, Guidance and Control</a> (whose sections are numbered 16.x there; 16.4.2.2 semiactive homing), 5 November 2026.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[3] Benchmark Sims, Falcon BMS 4.38.1 Training Manual.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-Training-Manual.pdf">PDF</a>, read 5 November 2026, pp. 182–194 and 231–243. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p id="source-a10"><strong>[4] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: A-10C II Tank Killer Flight Manual (English).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/dcs-warthog_2_flight_manual_en/">DCS documentation page</a>, read 5 November 2026, pp. 86, 89–90, 400, 620–623 and 676–681. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/precision-targeting-evidence.md">Precision Targeting evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 4 and the worked attack are original, invented examples. Figures 1 and 2 draw the zones of the joint publication's Figure III-9; Figure 4 is our geometry.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for aircrew of every service who employ laser-guided or coordinate-guided weapons, and the controllers who work with them. The procedures are from the joint publication, which all four services follow; no service's own weapons tactics publication could be read, so no service- or aircraft-specific tactics are included. Theater SPINS, your unit's tactics and your weapon's manual govern.</p>
